-- smucclaw/l4-ide#960 — a `WITH` supply mis-delivered across two same-spelled
-- section binders declared at DIFFERENT types.
--
-- Before the fix this file printed `Check succeeded.` and `#EVAL f` returned
-- the STRING "oops" from a rule declared `GIVETH A NUMBER`. Nothing was
-- reported at any stage, which is #956's exact observable in the one
-- configuration #956's fix (legalese/l4-ide#369) explicitly scoped out.
--
-- WHY IT GOT THROUGH. Two questions are answered in two different scopes and
-- nothing made them agree:
--
--   * WHICH BINDER'S TYPE IS THE VALUE CHECKED AGAINST?  `sectionBinderFor`
--     answers this, but only where the module has ONE binder of the spelling;
--     with two it returns `Nothing` — deliberately, see its Haddock — and
--     `implicitSupply` falls back to `resolveTerm`, which answers in the
--     CALLER's scope. Here the caller is `S3`, and it finds `S2`'s `rate`,
--     declared A STRING. "oops" checks against it fine.
--
--   * WHICH BINDER ACTUALLY RECEIVES THE VALUE?  `Discharge.suppliesBinder`
--     answers this, by matching the spelling against the CALLEE's READ-SET.
--     `g` reads `S1`'s `rate`, declared A NUMBER.
--
-- So the value was validated against `S2.rate` and delivered to `S1.rate`, and
-- no rule ever compared the two. The repair is a fourth whole-module check,
-- `Discharge.misdeliveredImplicitSupplies`, which can ask the question the type
-- checker cannot because the read-set — the very fact `sectionBinderFor` lacks
-- while a body is being checked — exists by the time it runs.
--
-- WHAT THIS FIXTURE PINS, and why each part is here:
--
--   1. The error fires at all, at the supply site (line 44, `rate` in the
--      `WITH`), not at either `GIVEN` line.
--   2. Both binders print SECTION-QUALIFIED — `S2.rate` and `S1.rate`. This is
--      load-bearing, not cosmetic: the whole content of the error is that two
--      DIFFERENT binders share one spelling, so a message printing them both
--      bare would name the problem and then hide it. Offering two candidates
--      under identical spellings is precisely the defect that sank the reverted
--      fix in legalese/l4-ide#369, and the golden is what keeps it from coming
--      back.
--   3. It fires on the DIFFERENT-TYPE case only. The same-typed pair is R3's
--      business (`not-ok/tc/section-given-root-ambiguous.l4`) and must keep
--      being refused there, by that error and not this one; widening this to
--      the spelling alone is the other reverted fix from #369.

IMPORT prelude

§ `S1`
    GIVEN rate IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
g MEANS rate

§ `S2`
    GIVEN rate IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
h MEANS rate

§ `S3`
GIVETH A NUMBER
f MEANS g WITH rate IS "oops"

#EVAL f
